title: Two layers, both of which must allow
subtitle: The coarse boundary and the fine one, and the switch that decides whether either is read
width: 1100
---
--- LAYER ONE: the service network policy --------------------------------

$ aws vpc-lattice get-auth-policy --resource-identifier <service-network-arn>

  state: Active                                                      [ok]

  "Sid": "SameOrgSameVpcOnly",
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": "*",
  "Condition": {
    "StringNotEquals": { "aws:PrincipalType": "Anonymous" },
    "StringEquals":    { "vpc-lattice-svcs:SourceVpc": "vpc-000b85..." }
  }

  Says nothing about WHICH caller. Only where a caller may come from, and
  that it must not be anonymous. The coarse boundary.

--- LAYER TWO: the service policy ----------------------------------------

$ aws vpc-lattice get-auth-policy --resource-identifier <service-arn>

  state: Active                                                      [ok]

  "Sid": "OnlyTheOrdersClientAndOnlyGet",
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<account>:role/...orders-client" },
  "Action": "vpc-lattice-svcs:Invoke",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:vpc-lattice:...:service/svc-0a1ee.../*",
  "Condition": {
    "StringEquals": { "vpc-lattice-svcs:RequestMethod": "GET" }
  }

  One role. One method. This is the line a security group cannot write.

--- AND THE SWITCH -------------------------------------------------------

$ aws vpc-lattice get-service --service-identifier <service-arn> \
      --query authType

  AWS_IAM

Three separate things: a policy on the network, a policy on the service,
and an authType on each deciding whether its policy is read at all.

Both policies must allow. Either authType set to NONE makes its layer wave
everything through -- and the policy stays attached, stays visible, and
quietly reports Inactive.
